# user/hkslot.ad — REPLACE THE KERNEL AN INSTALLED MACHINE BOOTS, FROM THE
# MACHINE, WITHOUT EVER LEAVING IT UNBOOTABLE.
#
# THE HOLE THIS FILLS, STATED FIRST
# =================================
# scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh's A/B layout (HAMLINUX_AB_SLOTS=1) made the kernel
# REPLACEABLE: the file firmware executes is systemd-boot, written once at
# build time and never rewritten, the kernel images sit beside it as
# \EFI\Linux\hamnix-a.efi and \EFI\Linux\hamnix-b.efi, and the only thing an
# update ever rewrites is a preallocated 512-byte \loader\loader.conf.
# tests/linux/ab_kernel_slots.sh measured that a machine really does boot the
# other slot afterwards, and that the ordering rule holds.
#
# AND NOTHING ON THE MACHINE COULD DO IT. That gate wrote the slot and flipped
# the pointer FROM THE HOST, with mcopy, against an image file. An installed
# machine had no program that could do either. This is that program.
#
# WHAT IT DOES, IN THE ORDER THE MEASUREMENT SAYS IT MUST
# ======================================================
#   1. Read \loader\loader.conf and find which slot is ACTIVE. The other one
#      is the target. Nothing is ever written to the slot this machine is
#      currently set to boot, so at every instant from here to the end there
#      is a whole, untouched, bootable kernel on the ESP.
#   2. Refuse unless the target slot ALREADY EXISTS at its full length. See
#      "NOTHING IS CREATED AND NOTHING IS EXTENDED" below.
#   3. Hash the source and compare it to the digest the caller was given out
#      of the SIGNED index. A kernel that does not match its digest is never
#      written anywhere.
#   4. Check the source is a PE image with a `.linux` section -- i.e. that it
#      is a kernel at all, and not, say, systemd-boot or a truncated download.
#   5. Write it into the inactive slot through an O_SYNC descriptor.
#   6. READ THE SLOT BACK OFF THE MEDIUM and hash what is actually there. It
#      must equal the same digest from step 3. Not the source's bytes in
#      memory -- the bytes on the disk.
#   7. ONLY THEN rewrite loader.conf, at exactly the length it already is.
#   8. Read loader.conf back and confirm it names the new slot.
#
# THE ORDER IS NOT A PREFERENCE, IT IS A MEASUREMENT. From
# tests/linux/ab_kernel_slots.sh, arms 6 and 7, on booted machines:
#
#   a HALF-WRITTEN INACTIVE SLOT with loader.conf still naming the old one
#       -> THE MACHINE BOOTS, on the old kernel.
#   the SAME torn slot with loader.conf ALREADY FLIPPED to it
#       -> THE MACHINE DOES NOT BOOT.
#
# Without the second of those the first proves nothing: it would be equally
# consistent with firmware that tolerates a torn image. It is not. So the flip
# is last, it follows a read-back, and every failure path in this program
# returns BEFORE the flip -- which is the state the first arm measured.
#
# NOTHING IS CREATED AND NOTHING IS EXTENDED, AND THAT IS THE SAFETY
# =================================================================
# Both slots are written at BUILD time, at full length. This program only ever
# OVERWRITES IN PLACE, through sys_open_sync -- which is O_WRONLY|O_SYNC with
# no O_CREAT and no O_TRUNC, and cannot create or truncate anything. So no
# cluster is ever allocated at update time, the FAT chain is never mutated,
# and no directory entry's length or starting cluster ever changes. FAT32 has
# no journal; the only reason that is survivable here is that the update path
# contains no metadata write at all. That is the identical argument
# user/bootlogd.ad makes for the preallocated boot log and user/bootsync.ad
# makes for its four-byte commit.
#
# It also means the refusal in step 2 is load bearing rather than pedantic. A
# medium built before the second slot was preallocated has an ESP with room
# for it and no file in it; creating one here would be a multi-hundred-megabyte
# FAT allocation on a filesystem with no journal, which is exactly the window
# this whole design exists to remove. So this refuses BY NAME and says the
# medium is too old, rather than doing the dangerous thing quietly.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
# ================================
# IT DOES NOT FETCH ANYTHING. It is handed a path and a digest. user/hpm.ad
# resolves the artifact out of the Ed25519-signed channel index -- the same
# trust path, the same signature check, the same verifier (lib/sha256.ad is
# now shared between the two for exactly this reason) that every package
# already goes through -- and then runs this. A program that both fetched and
# wrote the boot partition would be one program with the network in it and the
# ESP under it.
#
# IT DOES NOT TOUCH THE ACTIVE SLOT, EVER, on any path, including failure.
#
# IT DOES NOT REFRESH THE BOOT MODULES. That is user/bootsync.ad, and it is a
# different operation with a different failure mode; bootsync's own header
# says why mixing the two is how a module refresh ends up able to make a
# machine unbootable. After a slot flip the new slot's modules are whatever
# the new image shipped -- run bootsync afterwards to fold in the root's.
#
# Usage:
#   hkslot --status                   which slot is active, and the geometry
#   hkslot -n <uki> [<sha256hex>]     say what it would do; write nothing
#   hkslot <uki> [<sha256hex>]        write the inactive slot, verify, flip
#
# Exit: 0 committed (or -n / --status), 1 refused or failed. A non-zero exit
# from this program ALWAYS means loader.conf was not touched.

extern def sys_open(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_open_sync(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_read(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_close(fd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_lseek(fd: int32, offset: int64, whence: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_fd_size(fd: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_errstr(buf: Ptr[uint8], nbuf: uint64) -> int32

from lib.sha256 import (
    sha256_reset, sha256_update, sha256_final, sha256_hex,
)

SEEK_SET: int32 = 0

# THE PATHS ARE FIXED AND THEY ARE FUNCTIONS, NOT GLOBALS. Both halves matter.
#
# Fixed, because a path this program got wrong is a machine that does not
# boot, and there is no shell on it to find out from.
#
# Functions, because user/bootsync.ad paid a whole boot to learn that a string
# literal in a GLOBAL INITIALISER does not survive to runtime here while one in
# an expression does. Its failure was a program reporting "cannot read
# /boot/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI" about a file `cksum` read 67 MB of at the same
# shell in the same boot -- indistinguishable from a missing ESP.
def lconf_path() -> Ptr[char]:
    return cast[Ptr[char]]("/boot/loader/loader.conf")


def slot_a_path() -> Ptr[char]:
    return cast[Ptr[char]]("/boot/EFI/Linux/hamnix-a.efi")


def slot_b_path() -> Ptr[char]:
    return cast[Ptr[char]]("/boot/EFI/Linux/hamnix-b.efi")


# loader.conf's length is a CONTRACT with scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh: it is
# preallocated at this length and every rewrite is exactly this many bytes. A
# rewrite of a different length would allocate or free a cluster and mutate
# the FAT chain, which is the one thing this design must never do -- so the
# length is checked against the file before anything is written, and the
# program refuses rather than adapting to it.
LOADER_CONF_BYTES: uint64 = 512

IOBUF: uint64 = 262144
iobuf: Array[262144, uint8]
lbuf:  Array[1024, uint8]
cbuf:  Array[512, uint8]
errbuf: Array[160, uint8]
want_hex: Array[80, uint8]
src_hex:  Array[80, uint8]

# 0 = A is active (target B), 1 = B is active (target A).
active_b: int32 = 0


def strlen_u8(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    n: uint64 = 0
    while s[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    return n


def puts(fd: int32, s: Ptr[uint8]):
    sys_write(fd, s, strlen_u8(s))


def putdec(fd: int32, v: uint64):
    if v == 0:
        sys_write(fd, "0", 1)
        return
    d: Array[24, uint8]
    i: uint64 = 0
    while v > 0:
        d[i] = cast[uint8]((v % 10) + 48)
        v = v / 10
        i = i + 1
    while i > 0:
        i = i - 1
        sys_write(fd, &d[i], 1)


def why(what: Ptr[uint8]):
    n: int32 = sys_errstr(&errbuf[0], 158)
    puts(2, "hkslot: ")
    puts(2, what)
    if n > 0:
        puts(2, ": ")
        puts(2, &errbuf[0])
    puts(2, "\n")


def streq(a: Ptr[uint8], b: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while a[i] != 0 and b[i] != 0:
        if a[i] != b[i]:
            return 0
        i = i + 1
    if a[i] == b[i]:
        return 1
    return 0


def target_path() -> Ptr[char]:
    if active_b != 0:
        return slot_a_path()
    return slot_b_path()


def target_name() -> Ptr[uint8]:
    if active_b != 0:
        return cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamnix-a.efi")
    return cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamnix-b.efi")


def active_name() -> Ptr[uint8]:
    if active_b != 0:
        return cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamnix-b.efi")
    return cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamnix-a.efi")


# --- reading loader.conf ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Returns 0 and sets active_b when the file names one of the two slots, -1
# otherwise. A loader.conf that names something else is NOT quietly treated as
# slot A: this program would then write over the kernel the machine is
# actually booting. Refusing is the only safe answer to "I do not know which
# one is live".
def read_active() -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_open(lconf_path())
    if fd < 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: cannot read /boot/loader/loader.conf.\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: this machine is NOT on the A/B kernel layout -- either "
                "the ESP is\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: not bound at /boot (etc/rc.boot.installed does `bind "
                "'#esp' /boot`),\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: or it was installed from a medium built without "
                "HAMLINUX_AB_SLOTS=1.\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: Nothing was changed.\n")
        return -1
    n: int64 = sys_read(fd, &lbuf[0], 1023)
    sys_close(fd)
    if n <= 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: /boot/loader/loader.conf is empty. Refusing: this "
                "program cannot\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: tell which slot is live, and guessing would overwrite "
                "the running one.\n")
        return -1
    ln: uint64 = cast[uint64](n)
    lbuf[ln] = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < ln:
        s: uint64 = i
        while i < ln and lbuf[i] != 10:
            i = i + 1
        e: uint64 = i
        i = i + 1
        while s < e and (lbuf[s] == 32 or lbuf[s] == 9):
            s = s + 1
        if e - s < 8:
            continue
        if lbuf[s] != 100 or lbuf[s+1] != 101 or lbuf[s+2] != 102 \
           or lbuf[s+3] != 97 or lbuf[s+4] != 117 or lbuf[s+5] != 108 \
           or lbuf[s+6] != 116:
            continue
        p: uint64 = s + 7
        if lbuf[p] != 32 and lbuf[p] != 9:
            continue
        while p < e and (lbuf[p] == 32 or lbuf[p] == 9):
            p = p + 1
        q: uint64 = e
        while q > p and lbuf[q - 1] <= 32:
            q = q - 1
        if q <= p:
            continue
        lbuf[q] = 0
        if streq(&lbuf[p], cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamnix-a.efi")) != 0:
            active_b = 0
            return 0
        if streq(&lbuf[p], cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamnix-b.efi")) != 0:
            active_b = 1
            return 0
        puts(2, "hkslot: loader.conf's default is `")
        puts(2, &lbuf[p])
        puts(2, "', which is neither\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: hamnix-a.efi nor hamnix-b.efi. REFUSING -- this "
                "program cannot tell\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: which slot is live, and the wrong guess overwrites "
                "the running kernel.\n")
        return -1
    puts(2, "hkslot: /boot/loader/loader.conf has no `default' line. REFUSING "
            "for the\n")
    puts(2, "hkslot: same reason: sd-boot would DISCOVER an entry, and this "
            "program cannot\n")
    puts(2, "hkslot: predict which one it picks.\n")
    return -1


# --- the PE check ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# NOT a full validation -- it answers exactly one question: does this file
# contain a Linux kernel? A UKI has a `.linux` section; systemd-boot does not,
# and neither does a truncated download or an HTML error page a fetch wrote
# into a file. Pointing firmware at any of those costs the boot, and
# tests/linux/ab_kernel_slots.sh measured that it really does.
def rd_u16(b: Ptr[uint8], o: uint64) -> uint64:
    return cast[uint64](b[o]) + cast[uint64](b[o + 1]) * 256


def rd_u32(b: Ptr[uint8], o: uint64) -> uint64:
    return cast[uint64](b[o]) \
         + cast[uint64](b[o + 1]) * 256 \
         + cast[uint64](b[o + 2]) * 65536 \
         + cast[uint64](b[o + 3]) * 16777216


def read_at(fd: int32, off: uint64, buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int32:
    if sys_lseek(fd, cast[int64](off), SEEK_SET) < 0:
        return -1
    got: uint64 = 0
    while got < n:
        k: int64 = sys_read(fd, &buf[got], n - got)
        if k <= 0:
            return -1
        got = got + cast[uint64](k)
    return 0


def has_linux_section(fd: int32) -> int32:
    hdr: Array[8, uint8]
    if read_at(fd, 60, &hdr[0], 4) != 0:
        return 0
    lfa: uint64 = rd_u32(&hdr[0], 0)
    if lfa < 64 or lfa > 4096:
        return 0
    coff: Array[24, uint8]
    if read_at(fd, lfa, &coff[0], 24) != 0:
        return 0
    if coff[0] != 80 or coff[1] != 69 or coff[2] != 0 or coff[3] != 0:
        return 0
    nsec: uint64 = rd_u16(&coff[0], 6)
    optsz: uint64 = rd_u16(&coff[0], 20)
    if nsec == 0 or nsec > 64:
        return 0
    tab: uint64 = lfa + 24 + optsz
    i: uint64 = 0
    sh: Array[40, uint8]
    while i < nsec:
        if read_at(fd, tab + i * 40, &sh[0], 40) != 0:
            return 0
        # ".linux" -- eight bytes, NUL padded.
        if sh[0] == 46 and sh[1] == 108 and sh[2] == 105 and sh[3] == 110 \
           and sh[4] == 117 and sh[5] == 120 and sh[6] == 0:
            return 1
        i = i + 1
    return 0


# --- hashing ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Streaming, 256 KiB at a time. A UKI is around 176 MB and this program runs on
# every machine; a one-shot hash would have meant a 176 MB BSS array.
def hash_fd(fd: int32, n: uint64) -> int32:
    if sys_lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_SET) < 0:
        return -1
    sha256_reset()
    got: uint64 = 0
    while got < n:
        want: uint64 = n - got
        if want > IOBUF:
            want = IOBUF
        k: int64 = sys_read(fd, &iobuf[0], want)
        if k <= 0:
            return -1
        sha256_update(&iobuf[0], cast[uint64](k))
        got = got + cast[uint64](k)
    sha256_final()
    return 0


def copy_hex(dst: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < 64:
        dst[i] = sha256_hex[i]
        i = i + 1
    dst[64] = 0


def lower_hex_eq(a: Ptr[uint8], b: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < 64:
        ca: uint8 = a[i]
        cb: uint8 = b[i]
        if ca >= 65 and ca <= 70:
            ca = ca + 32
        if cb >= 65 and cb <= 70:
            cb = cb + 32
        if ca != cb:
            return 0
        i = i + 1
    return 1


# --- the flip --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# EXACTLY LOADER_CONF_BYTES BYTES, at offset 0, through an O_SYNC descriptor
# that cannot create and cannot truncate. The padding is '#' comment bytes,
# byte-for-byte the shape scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh wrote at build time, so the
# length never changes and neither does anything FAT records about the file.
def build_conf(name: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    lead: Ptr[uint8] = "timeout 0\ndefault "
    o: uint64 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while lead[i] != 0:
        cbuf[o] = lead[i]
        o = o + 1
        i = i + 1
    i = 0
    while name[i] != 0:
        cbuf[o] = name[i]
        o = o + 1
        i = i + 1
    tail: Ptr[uint8] = "\neditor no\n"
    i = 0
    while tail[i] != 0:
        cbuf[o] = tail[i]
        o = o + 1
        i = i + 1
    if o + 1 > LOADER_CONF_BYTES:
        return -1
    while o < LOADER_CONF_BYTES - 1:
        cbuf[o] = 35                                     # '#'
        o = o + 1
    cbuf[LOADER_CONF_BYTES - 1] = 10                     # '\n'
    return 0


def write_full(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int32:
    done: uint64 = 0
    while done < n:
        k: int64 = sys_write(fd, &buf[done], n - done)
        if k <= 0:
            return -1
        done = done + cast[uint64](k)
    return 0


def show_status() -> int32:
    if read_active() != 0:
        return 1
    puts(1, "hkslot: active slot   ")
    puts(1, active_name())
    puts(1, "\nhkslot: target slot   ")
    puts(1, target_name())
    puts(1, "\n")
    afd: int32 = sys_open(slot_a_path())
    if afd >= 0:
        puts(1, "hkslot: hamnix-a.efi  ")
        putdec(1, cast[uint64](sys_fd_size(afd)))
        puts(1, " bytes\n")
        sys_close(afd)
    else:
        puts(1, "hkslot: hamnix-a.efi  ABSENT\n")
    bfd: int32 = sys_open(slot_b_path())
    if bfd >= 0:
        puts(1, "hkslot: hamnix-b.efi  ")
        putdec(1, cast[uint64](sys_fd_size(bfd)))
        puts(1, " bytes\n")
        sys_close(bfd)
    else:
        puts(1, "hkslot: hamnix-b.efi  ABSENT -- this medium predates the "
                "preallocated second slot; hkslot will refuse to update it\n")
    lfd: int32 = sys_open(lconf_path())
    if lfd >= 0:
        puts(1, "hkslot: loader.conf   ")
        putdec(1, cast[uint64](sys_fd_size(lfd)))
        puts(1, " bytes (contract: ")
        putdec(1, LOADER_CONF_BYTES)
        puts(1, ")\n")
        sys_close(lfd)
    return 0


def main(argc: int32, argv: Ptr[uint64]) -> int32:
    dry: int32 = 0
    src: Ptr[uint8] = 0
    hexarg: Ptr[uint8] = 0
    ai: int32 = 1
    while ai < argc:
        a: Ptr[uint8] = cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[cast[uint64](ai)])
        if streq(a, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("-n")) != 0:
            dry = 1
        elif streq(a, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("--status")) != 0:
            return show_status()
        elif src == 0:
            src = a
        elif hexarg == 0:
            hexarg = a
        else:
            puts(2, "hkslot: usage: hkslot [-n] <uki> [<sha256hex>] | "
                    "hkslot --status\n")
            return 2
        ai = ai + 1
    if src == 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: usage: hkslot [-n] <uki> [<sha256hex>] | "
                "hkslot --status\n")
        return 2

    if hexarg != 0:
        if strlen_u8(hexarg) != 64:
            puts(2, "hkslot: the expected digest is not 64 hex characters. "
                    "REFUSING -- a\n")
            puts(2, "hkslot: malformed digest must not be treated as `no "
                    "digest given'.\n")
            return 1
        hi: uint64 = 0
        while hi < 64:
            want_hex[hi] = hexarg[hi]
            hi = hi + 1
        want_hex[64] = 0

    if read_active() != 0:
        return 1

    # --- the source ---------------------------------------------------------
    sfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](src))
    if sfd < 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: cannot read ")
        puts(2, src)
        puts(2, "\nhkslot: nothing was changed.\n")
        return 1
    ssz: int64 = sys_fd_size(sfd)
    if ssz <= 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: ")
        puts(2, src)
        puts(2, " is empty. Refusing.\n")
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1
    nsrc: uint64 = cast[uint64](ssz)
    if has_linux_section(sfd) == 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: ")
        puts(2, src)
        puts(2, " has no PE `.linux' section, so it is NOT a\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: kernel image. REFUSING. Pointing firmware at this "
                "would cost the boot\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: (tests/linux/ab_kernel_slots.sh arm II measured "
                "exactly that).\n")
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1

    # --- the target slot, which must ALREADY EXIST at full length -----------
    tfd_r: int32 = sys_open(target_path())
    if tfd_r < 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: the inactive slot ")
        puts(2, target_name())
        puts(2, " does not exist on this ESP.\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: REFUSING. Creating it here would be a "
                "several-hundred-megabyte FAT\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: allocation on a filesystem with no journal, which is "
                "the exact window\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: the A/B layout exists to remove. Both slots are "
                "preallocated at build\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: time by scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh; this medium "
                "predates that.\n")
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1
    tsz: int64 = sys_fd_size(tfd_r)
    if tsz <= 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: the inactive slot is zero bytes long. Refusing.\n")
        sys_close(tfd_r)
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1
    ntgt: uint64 = cast[uint64](tsz)
    if nsrc > ntgt:
        puts(2, "hkslot: the new kernel is ")
        putdec(2, nsrc)
        puts(2, " bytes and the slot preallocated for it\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: is ")
        putdec(2, ntgt)
        puts(2, " bytes. Writing it would EXTEND the file and allocate\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: clusters on a journal-less FAT32. REFUSING. Nothing "
                "was changed and\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: this machine boots exactly as it did before; it needs "
                "re-imaging from\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: a medium whose slots are big enough.\n")
        sys_close(tfd_r)
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1

    # --- the digest, checked BEFORE a single byte is written ----------------
    if hash_fd(sfd, nsrc) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hash the new kernel"))
        sys_close(tfd_r)
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1
    copy_hex(&src_hex[0])
    if hexarg != 0:
        if lower_hex_eq(&src_hex[0], &want_hex[0]) == 0:
            puts(2, "hkslot: THE NEW KERNEL DOES NOT MATCH ITS DIGEST.\n")
            puts(2, "hkslot:   signed index says ")
            puts(2, &want_hex[0])
            puts(2, "\nhkslot:   the file is      ")
            puts(2, &src_hex[0])
            puts(2, "\nhkslot: REFUSING. Nothing was written; this machine "
                    "boots exactly as it did.\n")
            sys_close(tfd_r)
            sys_close(sfd)
            return 1
        puts(1, "hkslot: the new kernel matches the digest from the signed "
                "index.\n")
    else:
        puts(2, "hkslot: WARNING: no expected digest given. The read-back "
                "below still proves\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: what LANDED equals what was READ, but nothing here "
                "proves the source\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: is what the repository signed. `hpm` always passes "
                "one.\n")

    puts(1, "hkslot: active ")
    puts(1, active_name())
    puts(1, ", writing ")
    putdec(1, nsrc)
    puts(1, " bytes into ")
    puts(1, target_name())
    puts(1, " (")
    putdec(1, ntgt)
    puts(1, " bytes preallocated)\n")

    if dry != 0:
        puts(1, "hkslot: -n given; nothing written, loader.conf untouched.\n")
        sys_close(tfd_r)
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 0

    # --- STEP 1: WRITE THE INACTIVE SLOT ------------------------------------
    #
    # sys_open_sync is O_WRONLY|O_SYNC and CANNOT create or truncate. If the
    # power goes here, what survives is a torn INACTIVE slot with loader.conf
    # still naming the live one -- the state arm I of
    # tests/linux/ab_kernel_slots.sh boots.
    wfd: int32 = sys_open_sync(target_path())
    if wfd < 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("open the inactive slot for writing"))
        puts(2, "hkslot: nothing was changed.\n")
        sys_close(tfd_r)
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1
    if sys_lseek(sfd, 0, SEEK_SET) < 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("rewind the new kernel"))
        sys_close(wfd)
        sys_close(tfd_r)
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1
    if sys_lseek(wfd, 0, SEEK_SET) < 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("rewind the inactive slot"))
        sys_close(wfd)
        sys_close(tfd_r)
        sys_close(sfd)
        return 1
    done: uint64 = 0
    # The guest prints this BEFORE the first byte of the slot and after
    # nothing else has been written, so a harness that wants to pull the power
    # in the middle of the copy has a marker that does not move with how long
    # the boot took. tests/linux/bootsync_installed.sh uses the same trick.
    puts(1, "hkslot: WRITING\n")
    while done < nsrc:
        want: uint64 = nsrc - done
        if want > IOBUF:
            want = IOBUF
        k: int64 = sys_read(sfd, &iobuf[0], want)
        if k <= 0:
            puts(2, "hkslot: the new kernel ended early at ")
            putdec(2, done)
            puts(2, " bytes.\n")
            puts(2, "hkslot: THE FLIP WAS NOT DONE, so this machine still "
                    "boots ")
            puts(2, active_name())
            puts(2, ".\n")
            sys_close(wfd)
            sys_close(tfd_r)
            sys_close(sfd)
            return 1
        if write_full(wfd, &iobuf[0], cast[uint64](k)) != 0:
            why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("write the inactive slot"))
            puts(2, "hkslot: THE FLIP WAS NOT DONE, so this machine still "
                    "boots ")
            puts(2, active_name())
            puts(2, ".\n")
            sys_close(wfd)
            sys_close(tfd_r)
            sys_close(sfd)
            return 1
        done = done + cast[uint64](k)
    sys_close(wfd)
    sys_close(sfd)
    puts(1, "hkslot: WROTE ")
    putdec(1, done)
    puts(1, " bytes; reading them back off the medium\n")

    # --- STEP 2: READ IT BACK OFF THE DISK ----------------------------------
    #
    # Off the MEDIUM, not out of the buffer that was just written. A write
    # that reported success and landed wrong has happened in this tree before
    # -- user/bootsync.ad's header records one that printed "committed" while
    # writing 16 MB into the middle of a live compressed archive.
    sys_close(tfd_r)
    vfd: int32 = sys_open(target_path())
    if vfd < 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("reopen the slot to verify it"))
        puts(2, "hkslot: THE FLIP WAS NOT DONE.\n")
        return 1
    if hash_fd(vfd, nsrc) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("read the slot back"))
        sys_close(vfd)
        puts(2, "hkslot: THE FLIP WAS NOT DONE.\n")
        return 1
    sys_close(vfd)
    if lower_hex_eq(&sha256_hex[0], &src_hex[0]) == 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: VERIFICATION FAILED -- what landed in ")
        puts(2, target_name())
        puts(2, " is not what\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: was written.  wrote ")
        puts(2, &src_hex[0])
        puts(2, "\nhkslot:                     read  ")
        puts(2, &sha256_hex[0])
        puts(2, "\nhkslot: loader.conf was NOT touched, so this machine boots ")
        puts(2, active_name())
        puts(2, " exactly\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: as it did a moment ago. Nothing is half-applied.\n")
        return 1
    puts(1, "hkslot: the slot reads back at ")
    puts(1, &src_hex[0])
    puts(1, "\n")

    # --- STEP 3: THE FLIP, AND IT IS LAST -----------------------------------
    lfd_r: int32 = sys_open(lconf_path())
    if lfd_r < 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("reopen loader.conf"))
        return 1
    lsz: int64 = sys_fd_size(lfd_r)
    sys_close(lfd_r)
    if cast[uint64](lsz) != LOADER_CONF_BYTES:
        puts(2, "hkslot: loader.conf is ")
        putdec(2, cast[uint64](lsz))
        puts(2, " bytes, not ")
        putdec(2, LOADER_CONF_BYTES)
        puts(2, ".\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: REFUSING to rewrite it: a write of a different length "
                "would allocate\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: or free a cluster and mutate the FAT chain. The new "
                "kernel is in the\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: inactive slot and this machine still boots ")
        puts(2, active_name())
        puts(2, ".\n")
        return 1
    if build_conf(target_name()) != 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: the new loader.conf does not fit in ")
        putdec(2, LOADER_CONF_BYTES)
        puts(2, " bytes.\n")
        return 1
    cfd: int32 = sys_open_sync(lconf_path())
    if cfd < 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("open loader.conf for writing"))
        puts(2, "hkslot: THE FLIP WAS NOT DONE; this machine boots ")
        puts(2, active_name())
        puts(2, ".\n")
        return 1
    if sys_lseek(cfd, 0, SEEK_SET) < 0 \
       or write_full(cfd, &cbuf[0], LOADER_CONF_BYTES) != 0:
        why(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("write loader.conf"))
        sys_close(cfd)
        # A torn 512-byte write is the worst this can leave, and
        # tests/linux/ab_kernel_slots.sh arm 8 measured the worst case of the
        # worst case: a loader.conf zeroed OUTRIGHT still boots, because
        # sd-boot falls back to discovering the entries.
        puts(2, "hkslot: loader.conf may be partially written. Both slots hold "
                "a whole\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: kernel, and a damaged loader.conf makes sd-boot "
                "discover the entries\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: rather than fail -- but run `hkslot --status` and "
                "check.\n")
        return 1
    sys_close(cfd)

    # --- STEP 4: read the flip back -----------------------------------------
    was_b: int32 = active_b
    if read_active() != 0:
        puts(2, "hkslot: loader.conf no longer parses after the flip.\n")
        return 1
    if active_b == was_b:
        puts(2, "hkslot: loader.conf STILL names ")
        puts(2, active_name())
        puts(2, " after the rewrite. The flip did\n")
        puts(2, "hkslot: not take. The next boot is the OLD kernel.\n")
        return 1
    puts(1, "hkslot: committed. loader.conf now names ")
    puts(1, active_name())
    puts(1, "; the next boot runs the new kernel.\n")
    puts(1, "hkslot: the previous kernel is still whole in the other slot.\n")
    return 0
